        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Druze faith</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1017 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, in Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamza-ibn-ali">Hamza ibn Ali</a> began in 1017 to preach a Muwaḥḥidūn (Unitarian) doctrine that developed from the Ismāʿīlī branch of Shiʿa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>; Wadi al-Taym in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> is generally considered the birthplace of the faith.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamza-ibn-ali">Hamza ibn Ali ibn Ahmad</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> mystic from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, considered its founder and the primary author of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>; the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim bi-Amr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> is its central figure, and al-Muqtana Baha’uddin led it after al-Hakim’s disappearance.</span></div>
