    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>, who call themselves al-Muwaḥḥidūn, “the monotheists”, hold to the strict unity of God, reincarnation and the eternity of the soul; their faith grew out of Ismāʿīlī <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, but most Druze do not identify as Muslims.</li><li>Since 1043 the faith has accepted no converts: one is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> only by birth to two Druze parents, and only the initiated ʻuqqāl read its holy books.</li><li>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> lords and fighters helped shape the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, while facing repeated persecution from Fatimid, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Ottoman rulers and modern extremists.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> still keep a ban on mulukhiyah that the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim bi-Amr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> imposed during his reign (996–1021): though it was lifted after him, they eat no mulukhiyah of any kind to this day.</p></aside>
    </section>

