    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faith arose in Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamza-ibn-ali">Hamza ibn Ali ibn Ahmad</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> mystic and scholar from Zozan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, came to Egypt in 1014 or 1016, gathered scholars who met in the Raydan Mosque near the Al-Hakim Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and in 1017 began to preach a Muwaḥḥidūn, or Unitarian, doctrine. He won the support of the sixth Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, al-Hakim bi-Amr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, who issued a decree promoting religious freedom and became the central figure of the faith. The movement first met in secret, in Sessions of Wisdom, and the divine call was opened on 30 May 1017.</p><p>A rival preacher, Muhammad bin Ismail Nashtakin ad-Darazi, had reached Cairo in 1015 or 1017, possibly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. He called himself “the Sword of the Faith” and taught that al-Hakim and his ancestors were the incarnation of God; Hamza warned him that “faith does not need a sword to aid it”. Riots broke out when al-Darazi read his book in Cairo’s principal mosque, and in 1018 al-Hakim had him executed. The Druze regard al-Darazi as a heretic, yet the name “Druze” probably derives from his; their own texts use only the word Muwaḥḥidūn.</p><p>The faith grew from the Ismāʿīlī branch of Shiʿa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and shows influences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, Gnosticism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> and Pythagoreanism. The historian David R. W. Bryer judged it as different from Islam as Islam is from Christianity. Most Druze trace their ancestry to twelve Arab tribes that settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> before and during the early Islamic period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> teaching God is one in strict and uncompromising unity, both transcendent and immanent: stripped of all attributes distinct from his essence, he is “the whole of existence”, without “how”, “when” or “where”. The Druze believe in tajalli, theophany, in which the light of God appears in the material realm without that realm becoming divine, as one is in a mirror without becoming the mirror. They hold that al-Hakim went into occultation and will return at the end of times to judge the world, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamza-ibn-ali">Hamza ibn Ali</a> is a reincarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the Universal Mind.</p><p>Reincarnation is a paramount principle. At death the soul passes at once into a new human body, a Druze into a Druze of the same sex, and the number of souls is finite; after successive rebirths it is united with the Cosmic Mind. The Druze believe that all of them signed, in past lives, the Pact of Time Custodian, the entrance to the faith. Seven precepts begin with truthfulness of speech and mutual aid among the brethren and end in submission to God’s will. Teachings are read on three levels, the exoteric, the esoteric and the “hidden of the hidden”, none abolishing the others.</p><p>Rituals are held to be symbolic, and most are not obligatory. Formal worship is a Thursday-evening meeting at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalwa">khalwa</a>, the prayer house, where the uninitiated leave after local affairs are discussed. Eid al-Adha is the most significant holiday, and pilgrims visit the shrine of Shu’ayb, revered as Jethro, between 25 and 28 April.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Epistles of Wisdom (Rasāʾil al-Ḥikma) are the foundational and central text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faith, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamza-ibn-ali">Hamza ibn Ali</a> is considered the primary author of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. The missionaries of the call carried the epistles with them and took written vows from believers, whose souls the Druze believe are still reborn in the Druze of today. The thirteenth epistle calls the faith “a spiritual doctrine without any ritualistic imposition”. Druze sacred texts also include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>; other early writings are the Rasa’il al-Hind (Epistles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>), didactic and polemic treatises, and manuscripts long lost or hidden, such as al-Munfarid bi-Dhatihi and al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> al-Ruhaniyya.</p><p>Only initiates have access to the holy books. The full canon is described as taking in the Old and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testaments</a>, the Quran and works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and of those influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, and Druze tradition counts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>, Plato and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> among the five great prophets of the age of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Some passages are hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>: an epistle ascribed to al-Muqtana, probably written between 1027 and 1042, accuses them of crucifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><p>In the Epistle of the Candle, Ismail at-Tamimi (d. 1030) listed the five limits, cosmic principles that the faith’s five colours stand for: green for the Universal Mind, red for the Universal Soul, yellow for the Word, blue for the anterior and white for the posterior. As stripes or a five-pointed star they are the Druze symbol, for the Druze strictly avoid iconography.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chouf"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chouf.jpg" alt="Chouf" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chouf_mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heretiq, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The call’s missionaries preached its message around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. After al-Hakim disappeared, the regent Sitt al-Mulk ordered the army to destroy the movement in 1021, and for seven years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faced extreme persecution under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Zahir, the mihna: tens of thousands were killed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, 5,000 prominent Druze at Antioch alone. The faith went underground, its survivors found mainly in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and it resumed in 1038; in 1043 al-Muqtana closed it to new pledges. Wadi al-Taym, one of the two main centres of the 11th-century mission, is generally considered its birthplace.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> rule (1099–1291) the Druze emerged into the full light of history in the Gharb region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a>, where the Tanukh family held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and the coastal plain against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. After the middle of the 12th century the Ma’an family, based at Baaqlin in the Chouf, took the lead. In 1305 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> defeated the Druze at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Keserwan</a>. Under Fakhr-al-Din II in the early 17th century Druze dominion stretched from the edge of the Antioch plain to Safad.</p><p>Druze moved east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a> in waves from 1685, and after the battle of Ain Dara in 1711 many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamani">Yamani</a> Druze followed, laying the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a> became the dominant group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>. Settlement in Jordan began in 1918, and in the 20th century emigrants took the faith to Venezuela, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and beyond.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> recognise no religious hierarchy and have no clergy. Those initiated into the holy books are the ʻuqqāl, “the wise”, men and women together making up less than 10% of the community; the rest are the juhhāl, “the ignorant”, a term seldom used, for which “spiritual” and “physical” are also said. Many ʻuqqāl wear the costume of the mountain people of earlier centuries, the women a loose white veil and the men white turbans that vary with seniority. The most influential become Ajawīd, from whom the spiritual leaders are drawn; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and Israel an elected Shaykh al-ʻAql heads each country’s religious council.</p><p>One is Druze only by birth to two Druze parents. Marriage outside the faith is strongly discouraged and seen as apostasy, and those who marry out may be ostracised and exiled from their villages. The Druze reject polygamy, discourage divorce, reject alcohol and nicotine, and follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> law where their own faith gives no ruling; many sheikhs remain unmarried. To protect the community they have practised taqiyya, concealing their beliefs, at times posing as Muslims or Christians.</p><p>The Druze were persecuted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> Eyalet, through massacres, the demolition of prayer houses and forced conversion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taymiyya">Ibn Taymiyya</a> ruled that they should be killed and their women enslaved, and the Ottomans often relied on his ruling. Today Lebanon, Syria, Israel and Jordan recognise the Druze as a separate religious community with its own courts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash.jpg" alt="Sultan al-Atrash" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash">Sultan al-Atrash</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Pasha_Al-Atrash2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Colony (Jerusalem) Photography Dept. (photographer unnamed), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>, between 800,000 and a million people, are one of the major religious groups of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>: 5.5% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>’s population, 3% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>’s and 1.6% of Israel’s, with smaller communities in Jordan. The oldest and densest communities are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a> in southern Syria. Arabic is their language and Arab culture part of their identity, and their political weight in the region has long exceeded their numbers.</p><p>The Ma’an and Shihab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled Mount Lebanon, and after the civil conflict of 1860, which cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a> some ten thousand lives, the Maronite-Druze dualism of the autonomous Mutasarrifate brought one of Lebanon’s calmest periods. In Syria <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash">Sultan Pasha al-Atrash</a> led the Druze in the Syrian Revolution of 1925–27, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adib-shishakli">Adib Shishakli</a>’s government attacked the Jabal al-Druze in the early 1950s, and in 2018 an ISIS-affiliated attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwayda">Suwayda</a> killed at least 258 people. In Lebanon most Druze supported Kamal Jumblatt’s Progressive Socialist Party. Israel has recognised the Druze as a distinct community since 1957 and its Druze men serve in the army, though the Druze scholars Kais Firro and Rabah Halabi call the “covenant of blood” between Druze and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> a myth.</p><p>Abroad, Venezuela has about 60,000 Druze and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> 30,000 to 50,000, where finding a Druze partner and keeping the faith alive are pressing concerns.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faith arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1017, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamza-ibn-ali">Hamza ibn Ali</a> preached a strict Unitarian doctrine under the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim, and it has accepted no converts since 1043. Its followers, al-Muwaḥḥidūn, believe in one God, theophany and reincarnation, keep their scripture, the Epistles of Wisdom, among initiates, and live mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, Israel and Jordan.</em></p>

